"Untitled Document (https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22511v1)\nciteturn41227view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/html/2610.22511v1\",\"lineno\":315}); Total lines: 1079\nL235: # {'arc_easy:acc': 0.61, 'hellaswag:acc_norm': 0.58, 'gsm8k:exact_match': 0.34, ...}\nL236: ### 5.2  Experiment management\nL237: evaluate_many() evaluates one model on several datasets (Listing cite110†11 ); YAML and the CLI expose the same operations (Appendix cite53†A ). ak.generate() stores outputs once so that model=\"precomputed\" can re-score them under other metrics, each scoring configuration receiving its own fingerprint. ExperimentDB keeps a queryable history, optionally mirrored to MLflow [cite84†49 ].\nL238: When a model or dataset folder carries lexsi_provenance.json, AuditKIT records it in RunResult.metadata[\"inputs\"], writes it next to saved results, and uploads it with push_to_hub, so a result traces back to its checkpoint and data.\nL239: ## 6  Metric Library, Custom Metrics, and Direction-Aware Comparison\nL240: ### 6.1  The metric contract and custom metrics\nL241: \nL242: Every metric implements score(sample, output, context) -> Score and declares a direction; omitting it raises TypeError. A function decorated with @ak.scorer(direction=...) (Listing cite109†4 ) or a full Metric subclass (Listing cite111†15 ) is scored, aggregated, and compared exactly like a built-in.\nL243: \nL244: Listing 4: A custom metric, registered and used identically to a built-in metric.\nL245: \nL246: ⬇\nL247: \nL248: import auditkit as ak\nL249: # direction= is required: omitting it raises TypeError at class-definition\nL250: \nL251: # time (Section 4.1), so a winner is never picked from an undeclared metric.\nL252: \nL253: @ak.scorer(direction=ak.Direction.MAXIMIZE)\nL254: \nL255: def mentions_price(sample, output) -> float:\nL256: \nL257: return 1.0 if \"$\" in output else 0.0\nL258: \nL259: result = ak.evaluate(\nL260: \nL261: samples, model=\"openai:gpt-4o-mini\",\nL262: \nL263: # Custom scorer and a built-in metric name side by side: same list, same code path.\nL264: \nL265: scorers=[mentions_price, \"exact_match\"],\nL266: \nL267: )\nL268: ### 6.2  Supported metrics\nL269: Table cite112†3 summarizes the built-in families. The generation family implements BLEU [cite113†30 ], ROUGE [cite114†23 ], and BERTScore [cite115†50 ]; GEval implements G-Eval [cite116†25 ]. The four lexical RAG metrics are named for the signals they compute, not after RAGAS [cite79†9 ]; the judged RAG family does claim-level judging and is kept separate so the two methodologies are never averaged under one name. The agent, retrieval, and deterministic RAG families are detailed in Section cite32†7 and Table cite117†4 .\nL270: Table 3: Built-in metric families, representative metrics, and registry counts (registered implementations, not independently validated metrics).\nL271: Family  | Representative metrics  | What it measures  | #\nL272: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL273: Built-in  | ExactMatch, QuasiExactMatch, Acc, AccNorm  | Exact / normalized correctness against a target  | 4\nL274: Code / deterministic  | Contains, Regex, IsJson, F1Score, Levenshtein, Equals, StartsWith, EndsWith, WordCount  | Structural or exact string/JSON correctness  | 9\nL275: Generation quality  | Bleu, RogueL (registry name rouge_l), ChrF [cite118†32 ], WordErrorRate, Perplexity, BertScore  | N-gram overlap, fluency, LM confidence  | 6\nL276: Embedding similarity  | CosineSimilarity, TokenOverlap, BM25Similarity  | Semantic and lexical similarity  | 3\nL277: Retrieval-augmented generation (RAG)  | LexicalGroundedness, ContextOverlap, ContextCoverage, AnswerOverlap  | Groundedness and retrieval quality (lexical-overlap heuristic)  | 4\nL278: Hallucination  | FactualConsistency  | Groundedness of generated claims against source text  | 1\nL279: Toxicity / bias  | ToxicityScore, RepresentationSkew, HateSpeechScore  | Content-safety signals  | 3\nL280: Pairwise / preference  | WinRate, EloScore, PreferenceAccuracy  | Model-vs-model preference comparison  | 3\nL281: Security  | KeywordDetector  | Presence of unsafe keywords/patterns  | 1\nL282: LLM-as-judge  | LLMJudge, GEval (weighted rubric), Factuality, ClosedQA, Relevance, BiasJudge  | Rubric- or criteria-based judging by another LLM  | 6\nL283: Encoder-judge  | EncoderJudge, FactualityEncoderJudge, SentimentEncoderJudge  | Rubric-style judging by a lightweight encoder classifier, not a generative LLM  | 3\nL284: Guardrail  | GuardJudge  | Content-moderation verdict from a dedicated guard model  | 1\nL285: Agent / tool use  | ToolCallF1, TrajectoryMatch, ParallelToolCalls, ToolCallValidity, RedundantToolCalls, AgentLoopDetection, ToolPermission, TaskCompletion, ToolSelectionJudge  | Call correctness, turn structure, parallel-call behavior, schema validity, redundancy, loops, least privilege, judged completion  | 9\nL286: Retrieval & judged RAG  | RetrievalMetrics, Faithfulness, ContextPrecision, ContextRecall, AnswerRelevancy, ResponseGroundedness, Hallucination, ContextRelevance  | Ranked retrieval quality; claim- and sentence-level grounding judged by an LLM, with and without a reference  | 8\nL287: Deterministic RAG checks  | EvidenceSetRecall, Freshness, AclCompliance, CitationSupport, Abstention, NumericAccuracy, ContextRetention  | Model-free checks of evidence completeness, freshness, access control, citations, numbers, and abstention against a versioned corpus  | 7\nL288: Total  |  |  | 68\nL289: Performance measurements attach to every RunResult automatically. Some metrics emit more than one score, so registry size describes the available metrics rather than the number of result fields.\nL290: ## 7  Evaluating Agentic and Retrieval-Augmented Systems\nL291: \nL292: AuditKIT evaluates agents at two levels that share one set of metric classes. At the metric level, tool-calling models, deployed agents, and RAG pipelines go through evaluate() like any other task. At the episode level, auditkit.agent_eval evaluates a whole task with a verified outcome as the headline. Table cite117†4 lists the metrics; the whole layer is stdlib-only and never imports AgentTune, torch, or transformers.\nL293: Table 4: Agent and RAG metrics with their required inputs.\nL294: Metric Needs Measures Agent / tool use tool_call_f1 reference calls precision, recall, F1, exact set; maximum bipartite matching trajectory_match reference turns agreement between the predicted and reference tool-call sequences parallel_tool_calls reference turns independent calls batched (recall), dependent calls not (precision), when to batch (detection) tool_call_validity offered schemas calls valid against the schemas redundant_tool_calls $\\downarrow$ trace exact repeats of an earlier call agent_loop_detection trace repeated calls, stagnating messages, call-graph cycles tool_permission allow / deny list calls within a least-privilege policy task_completion, tool_selection judge model judged completion; judged justification of each call Retrieval and judged RAG retrieval$(k)$ gold contexts, ranked retrieval hit rate, precision, recall, MRR, AP, nDCG faithfulness, context_precision, context_recall contexts (+ reference) claims supported; relevant chunks ranked first; reference covered answer_relevancy, response_groundedness, hallucination $\\downarrow$, context_relevance question, contexts, answer; no reference reference-free relevance and grounding Deterministic RAG checks (model-free) evidence_set_recall, context_retention case evidence sets, trace a complete sufficient evidence set retrieved; still present in the final context freshness, acl_compliance corpus snapshot, case date and identity no superseded documents; no unauthorized or cross-tenant documents citation_support, numeric_accuracy, abstention gold citations, gold answer, answerability cited spans exist and support; numbers correct; abstains exactly when it should\nL295: ### 7.1  Trace representation\nL296: A reference is a list of turns, each the set of calls made in one response: [[a, b], [c]] says a and b are independent and belong together, while c depends on them; [] means no tool should be called. Calls may be {name, arguments} records, OpenAI tool_calls, or BFCL’s form; arguments that do not decode mark the call as unmatched. The trace comes from native tool calls, a deployed agent: endpoint, a recorded run under precomputed, or, failing those, the parsed text (Listing cite119†16 ).\nL297: When a trace marks tool coverage unavailable, the reference metrics are ineligible rather than scored as “no call”.\nL298: ### 7.2  Tool-call and trajectory metrics\nL299: Let the reference be turns $R=(R_{1},\\dots,R_{m})$ and the run $P=(P_{1},\\dots,P_{n})$, and $\\mathcal{C}(\\cdot)$ the multiset of all calls. A predicate $\\mu(p,r)$ requires equal tool names and, for arguments, equality (\"exact\"), reference-containment (\"subset\": every reference argument must appear in the call, with extra call arguments allowed), or no argument check (\"name\"), or a custom arg_match.\nL300: Calls are paired by a maximum bipartite matching $M$ (Hungarian-style augmenting paths [cite95†16 ]), so a greedy first fit never rejects a correct run. Precision is $|M|/|\\mathcal{C}(P)|$ and recall $|M|/|\\mathcal{C}(R)|$. With $G=\\{R_{i}:|R_{i}|\\geq 2\\}$ the parallel groups and $Q$ the predicted turns holding at least two matched calls,\nL301:  | par. recall  | $\\displaystyle=\\tfrac{1}{|G|}\\textstyle\\sum_{g\\in G}\\mathbf{1}\\big[\\exists j:\\ g\\text{ fully matched in }P_{j}\\big],$  |\nL302:  | par. precision  | $\\displaystyle=\\tfrac{1}{|Q|}\\big|\\{P_{j}\\in Q:\\ \\text{the matched calls of }P_{j}\\text{ come from one }R_{i}\\}\\big|,$  |\nL303: and detection is $\\mathbf{1}[(\\exists j:|P_{j}|\\geq 2)=(\\exists i:|R_{i}|\\geq 2)]$. Low recall means independent calls were serialized; low precision means dependent calls were batched before their inputs existed (Listing cite120†5 ). These scores measure adherence to the reference’s turn structure, not task success: serializing independent calls can be functionally correct, and a trace of calls does not show that their results were used. Scores without a denominator are omitted, not zeroed.\nL304: Listing 5: Parallel-call scoring: three runs with identical call-level F1 are separated by turn structure. Real output.\nL305: \nL306: ⬇\nL307: \nL308: import auditkit as ak\nL309: \nL310: W = lambda city: {\"name\": \"get_weather\", \"arguments\": {\"city\": city}}\nL311: \nL312: BOOK = {\"name\": \"book_flight\", \"arguments\": {\"flight_id\": \"AZ-61\"}}\nL313: \nL314: REF = [[W(\"Paris\"), W(\"Rome\")], [BOOK]] # a parallel group, then a dependent call\nL315: \nL316: runs = {\"correct\": [[W(\"Paris\"), W(\"Rome\")], [BOOK]],\nL317: \nL318: \"serialized\": [[W(\"Paris\")], [W(\"Rome\")], [BOOK]],\nL319: \"batched\": [[W(\"Paris\"), W(\"Rome\"), BOOK]]}\nL320: \nL321: for name, turns in runs.items():\nL322: \nL323: s = ak.Sample(input=\"Which is warmer, Paris or Rome? Book AZ-61 if it's Rome.\",\nL324: \nL325: expected_tool_calls=REF, actual_output=\"\", actual_trace={\"tool_calls\": turns})\nL326: \nL327: h = ak.evaluate([s], model=\"precomputed\",\nL328: \nL329: scorers=[ak.ToolCallF1(), ak.ParallelToolCalls()]).headline\nL330: \nL331: print(name, h[\"tool_call_f1\"], h[\"parallel_recall\"], h.get(\"parallel_precision\"))\nL332: # correct 1.0 1.0 1.0\nL333: \nL334: # serialized 1.0 0.0 None <- independent calls split across turns\nL335: # batched 1.0 1.0 0.0 <- booked before the weather result existed\nL336: A reference may list several valid routes, {\"any_of\": [...]}. One route is chosen per sample by the key (F1, recall, exact turn structure, in-order coverage, listing order), and every reference metric scores that same route, so a row never describes two different runs; scores record the chosen route and per-route F1, and RunResult.route_distribution() reports which rule decided.\nL337: Because the route is chosen with the prediction in view, these scores measure best-route reference compatibility, an optimistic measure, not independent evidence of task success. Reference-free checks need only the trace, and ak.load_bfcl() with bfcl_arg_match reimplements BFCL’s accepted-value rules. A non-default argument mode or custom matcher suffixes the score name, so differently configured instances are never averaged together.\nL338: ### 7.3  Task outcomes and reliability\nL339: \nL340: An AgentCase states the task, allowed tools, budgets, and an outcome oracle; an AgentEpisode is one run as ordered events (with call and turn ids) plus final answer, state, and artifacts (Figure cite97†2 ). Every imported episode reports, for nine fields, whether each was observed, inferred, or unavailable, and a scorer runs only when its fields were observed; otherwise the report names the missing field.\nL341: #### Oracles.\nL342: Oracles return success, failure, unknown, or error: FinalStateAssertion and ArtifactAssertion check a real effect, in the spirit of $\\tau$-bench; AnswerAssertion checks the answer; CustomPredicate runs versioned caller code; and AssertionOracle turns a natural-language criterion into a verdict via a held-out judge, after an optional state check.\nL343: Judges are useful but imperfect [cite121†51 ], so TaskCompletion is a diagnostic that never overrides a verified oracle, and disagreements between judge and oracle are recorded. A verdict is relative to the specified oracle and the observed state, so its reliability depends on the oracle’s correctness and coverage. A producer’s own reward is kept as provenance only.\nL344: #### Execution modes.\nL345: \nL346: Runs are recorded (no calls), deployed (an agent: endpoint under a wall-clock deadline), or harness (AuditKIT drives a policy against caller-supplied tool doubles). Each case ends in one of seven statuses; the summary gives success over all cases and over decided cases.\nL347: #### Reliability.\nL348: \nL349: With $N$ trials, the headline is the majority of decided trials (a tie is unknown). The report also gives\nL350: \nL351:  | $$\\text{pass@}k=1-\\binom{n-c}{k}\\Big/\\binom{n}{k},\\qquad\\text{all-}k=\\binom{c}{k}\\Big/\\binom{n}{k},$$  |\nL352: \nL353: where $\\text{pass@}k$ follows Chen et al. [cite122†4 ] and $\\text{all-}k$ is the pass^{k} of $\\tau$-bench, flagged unless the caller confirms a reset between trials. rescore() replays saved episodes under new scorers without endpoint calls (Listing cite123†6 ).\nL354: Figure 2: Episode-level agent evaluation separates verified outcomes from trace diagnostics. Dashed arrows mark caller-supplied artifact links. Listing 6: Episode evaluation: a final-state oracle is the headline; tool scores are diagnostics; nothing is called.\nL355: \nL356: ⬇\nL357: \nL358: from auditkit.agent_eval import (AgentCase, AgentEvalRunner, AgentEvalSpec,\nL359: \nL360: FinalStateAssertion, episode_from_openai_messages)\nL361: \nL362: episode = episode_from_openai_messages(messages, case_id=\"inventory-001\",\nL363: \nL364: final_answer=\"Shipment for A is ready.\")\nL365: episode.final_state = {\"shipment_decision\": \"ready\"} # the verified effect\nL366: \nL367: case = AgentCase(id=\"inventory-001\", task=\"Ship item A if it is in stock.\",\nL368: \nL369: allowed_tools=[\"lookup_inventory\"],\nL370: \nL371: outcome=FinalStateAssertion(\"shipment_decision\", equals=\"ready\"))\nL372: \nL373: result = AgentEvalRunner().run(AgentEvalSpec(\nL374: \nL375: cases=[case], mode=\"recorded\", episodes=[episode], trials=1,\nL376: \nL377: scorers=[\"tool_call_f1\", \"tool_call_validity\"])) # add judge=... for task_completion\nL378: # Verified success: 1/1 (1/1 decided) Failure: 0 Unknown outcome: 0 ...\nL379: ### 7.4  Retrieval-augmented generation\nL380: A RAG pipeline is scored on what it actually retrieved: the trace’s contexts when returned, the dataset’s otherwise. RetrievalMetrics(k) is deterministic; its average precision divides by all relevant items, so an unretrieved gold chunk lowers the score, and its ideal DCG [cite124†15 ] comes from the gold grades. Judged metrics work with any text judge and treat an unparseable verdict as an error; faithfulness fails unsupported claims, not only contradicted ones (Listing cite125†18 ).\nL381: In regulated settings such as banking, a grounded answer can still rest on a stale, unauthorized, or misattributed source.\nL382: Seven model-free checks read a versioned CorpusSnapshot, a RAGCase (sufficient evidence sets, citations, tenant, decision date), and a RAGTrace, and each returns a score or an explicit unknown: a complete evidence set was retrieved and kept in the final context, the documents were current at the decision date, retrieval stayed within the caller’s access rights, citations exist and support the answer, the numbers are right, and the system abstained exactly when the question was unanswerable.\nL383: ### 7.5  Integration with external artifacts\nL384: AuditKIT reads AgentTune [cite73†28 ] artifacts by record shape, never by import, and sets coverage from what each artifact contains (Table cite126†5 ); a serialized EventLog’s turn boundaries become parallel groups, and AgentTune’s healing-audit records, a per-call projection rather than a trajectory, are not imported.\nL385: Several AgentTune metrics map onto AuditKIT in stricter form: argument correctness to tool_call_validity, redundancy ratio to redundant_tool_calls, loop frequency to agent_loop_detection, tool-chain order to trajectory_in_order, and message-id recall to retrieval recall; self-correction, tool efficacy, plan adherence, and path edit distance have no counterpart yet.\nL386: AuditKIT never writes into AgentTune’s data: write_sidecar() emits verdicts, coverage, and reliability keyed by trajectory id (Listing cite127†19 ).\nL387: Table 5: AgentTune artifact fields and the diagnostics available from them.\nL388: AgentTune artifact AuditKIT entry point What is observed Eligible without annotation Evaluation / RL dataset rows (prompt, gold answer, gold chunk ids) load_agenttune a task to run: input, target, gold contexts, and the original system prompt; no events yet answer metrics; retrieval once a live run returns ids; task_completion Trajectory JSONL (steps) load_agenttune, episodes_from_agenttune tool calls per step with arguments when recorded; conversation; retrieved chunk ids; reward as provenance redundancy, loop detection, task_completion; reference metrics once a reference is attached Training trace (trace.jsonl) same final answer and retrieved ids; calls carry no tool names answer metrics, retrieval, task_completion Evaluation report JSON same answers plus flattened tool names only (tool_names_only); report metadata kept tool_call_f1_name, tool_call_validity, task_completion Serialized EventLog episode_from_eventlog ordered events, turn grouping (full tier), call ids, reward as provenance all trajectory metrics the recorded tier supports Healing audit records refused (named error) a lossy per-call projection none\nL389: The same read-only pattern covers AlignTune [cite74†27 ] (scorecards and regression reports), SafeTune [cite75†40 ] (policy events and benchmark rows), CuratorKIT [cite76†2 ] (samples with provenance, manifests, trainer exports), and CircuitKIT [cite77†39 ] (circuit faithfulness reports). Each artifact is wrapped in a SourceEvidence envelope listing the join keys present and missing, and join_evidence() attaches it to an episode only through ids the caller names, refusing to pin aggregate evidence to one case.\nL390: ## 8  Empirical Studies\nL391: Each study is a single run or a small or controlled sample (the sample size $N$, from 6 to 100, is stated with each). They illustrate what the library reports and distinguishes rather than general model rankings. Studies 1 to 3 ran on the earlier auditkit 0.3.0 line (44 metrics, 7 adapters, no agent layer, not published on PyPI); Studies 4 to 7 used the 1.1.2 release, Study 4 through its vLLM and SGLang integration code.\nL392: All runs use greedy decoding, with output budgets (max_tokens) of 8 for Study 1, 300 for Study 2, 100 for Study 3 (200 for its judge), 64 for Study 5, and 512 for Study 7. Studies 1 to 3 cover arbitrary metrics, direction-correct multi-model comparison, and performance measurement. Study 4 serves one model family through four runtimes.\nL393: Studies 5 to 7 apply the RAG, interoperability, and tool-calling layers: a multi-hop question-answering assistant, artifacts from the Lexsi Labs stack, and the choice of a tool-calling model. Studies 5 and 6 are the smallest and illustrate what each layer reports.\nL394: ### 8.1  Study 1: Evaluating pruned models\nL395: We evaluate three community-pruned MLP-neuron variants of Llama-3.2-3B [cite128†10 ] (the third-party oopere/pruned{20,40,60}-llama-3.2-3b checkpoints, whose pruning method and any post-pruning recovery are the checkpoint author’s, not ours) against the unpruned base model on 100 BoolQ [cite129†5 ] questions, one run per severity and without repeated seeds. The baseline is the base (not instruct) model with a one-shot prompt, and the answer is a RegexAnnotator match on ^\\s*(yes|no).\nL396: Each variant is compared with the baseline through RunComparison.retention() (candidate $\\div$ baseline) on three metrics: boolq_answer_match (exact-match answer scoring), not_degenerate_output (flags only three identical consecutive words; it does not detect empty output), and factual_correctness_judge (LLMJudge-based).\nL397: Table 6: Relative metric retention by pruning severity. Baseline absolute scores were not retained, so the ratios are reported as relative retention only.\nL398: \nL399: Pruning severity boolq_answer_match ($\\uparrow$) not_degenerate_output ($\\uparrow$) factual_correctness_judge ($\\uparrow$) 20% 0.411 1.000 0.385 40% 0.726 1.000 0.788 60% 0.630 1.000 0.673\nL400: Retention is non-monotonic across severities, and every variant fails the predefined threshold. The flat non-degeneracy score is a coarse signal, because the output budget is short and the heuristic misses empty outputs. The baseline’s absolute scores were not retained and may be near chance, so the ratios describe relative retention rather than a pruning effect. Model size falls with severity, which confirms the pruning took effect.\nL401: ### 8.2  Study 2: Comparing language models\nL402: \nL403: We run compare_models() over three local Hugging Face checkpoints (Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct [cite128†10 ], Llama-2-13b-chat-hf [cite130†11 ], Qwen/Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct [cite131†35 ]; via HFGenModel) on 100 SST-2 [cite132†41 ] sentiment samples with QuasiExactMatch, reporting per-model accuracy, latency, throughput, and model size (Table cite133†7 ).\nL404: \nL405: Table 7: Model scores and whole-run time on SST-2; run time is a whole-run measurement, not per-request latency.\nL406: Model quasi_exact_match ($\\uparrow$) Params Size (MiB) Run time (ms, $\\downarrow$) llama-8b (Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct) 0.930 8.03B 15,316.5 6,601 llama-13b (Llama-2-13b-chat-hf) 0.580 13.02B 24,825.8 32,388 qwen-14b (Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct) 0.900 14.77B 28,171.6 106,512\nL407: Adding regex extraction changes the apparent ranking even though the generated outputs are unchanged: the model that scores lowest without extraction becomes competitive after its labels are parsed. This shows how answer formatting can masquerade as a model-quality difference.\nL408: Whole-run wall time is not per-request latency and varied substantially between runs, so it is not used for a speed ranking. Per-sample scores were not retained, so we reconstructed confidence intervals and significance tests from aggregate accuracies; the paired test that suits these repeated examples is unavailable. The post-extraction differences are not statistically significant on these aggregates, and equivalence of the models is not tested.\nL409: The study shows that generation, annotation, scoring, and performance measurement can be varied independently under one common RunResult.\nL410: Table 8: Scores after regex extraction; timings are whole-run measurements from a separate run.\nL411: \nL412: Model quasi_exact_match ($\\uparrow$) Std. dev. $n$ Run time (ms, $\\downarrow$, this run) llama-8b (Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct) 0.930 (unchanged) 0.2551 100 64,861 llama-13b (Llama-2-13b-chat-hf) 0.910 (was 0.580) 0.2862 100 112,349 qwen-14b (Qwen2.5-14B-Instruct) 0.960 (was 0.900) 0.1960 100 107,349\nL413: ### 8.3  Study 3: Financial question answering\nL414: Financial-filing question answering needs the right number, not a plausible sentence, so numeric correctness and semantic grounding fail differently. We run compare_models() on 100 question/answer/context triplets from virattt/financial-qa-10K [cite134†44 ]; the first 100 rows are all NVIDIA (NVDA), so this is a single-company case study. It compares Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct [cite128†10 ] (fp16) with Llama-2-13b-chat-hf [cite130†11 ], judged by an independent Meta-Llama-3-8B-Instruct [cite128†10 ].\nL415: Four scorers run per sample: NumericFactMatch (a notebook-defined heuristic that checks whether the first extracted number equals the first reference number, and breaks on unit changes such as “$26.97 billion” vs “26,974 million”), LexicalGroundedness and BertScore (grounding and semantic similarity against the filing excerpt), and factual_correctness_judge.\nL416: The judge is in the same family as llama-8b, so a self-preference effect is possible. Llama-2-13b-chat-hf and the judge were loaded 4-bit quantized to fit beside the unquantized 8B model on one GPU. This confounds the 8B-vs-13B comparison, because quantization can affect accuracy and run time as well as size: llama-13b’s reported size, 6,675.8 MiB, is smaller than llama-8b’s fp16 15,316.5 MiB despite more parameters.\nL417: Table 9: Metric scores on questions from NVIDIA’s 2023 10-K filing. The 13B model and the judge ran 4-bit quantized, and numeric_fact_match compares only the first extracted number.\nL418: \nL419: Model numeric_fact_match ($\\uparrow$) lexical_groundedness ($\\uparrow$) bert_score ($\\uparrow$) factual_correctness_judge ($\\uparrow$) llama-8b (fp16) 0.3800 0.6659 0.8685 0.8300 llama-13b (4-bit) 0.4100 0.5812 0.7936 0.8700\nL420: The metrics disagree: one model leads on grounding, while the quantized larger model leads on numeric matching and the judge score. The latter gaps overlap their uncertainty intervals, so they are not used for a ranking. The case shows why metric disagreement and evaluation setup, including quantization and judge family, should be reported together.\nL421: ### 8.4  Study 4: Evaluation across serving runtimes\nL422: We run nine Cohere models [cite135†6 ] through four routes: hf:, offline vllm:, SGLang via api:, and the Hugging Face router via api:. Each model answers one factual question in five languages (PASS $\\geq$ 4/5), on a 40 GB A100 or 95 GB RTX PRO 6000. The vllm: column ran vLLM 0.30.0 with transformers 5.16.1, the SGLang column SGLang 0.5.20, and the hf: column transformers 5.17, measured between 2026-09-30 and 2026-10-01; all use greedy decoding.\nL423: Image tests in the vllm: column use vllm serve through api:; offline vllm: does not accept images. Superscripts mark Transformers backends, SGLang threshold reporting, or matching answers across routes.\nL424: Table 10: Correct answers out of five language probes by model and serving route. These are compatibility probes, not estimates of multilingual performance.\nL425: Model Architecture hf: vllm: SGLang via api: HF router via api: Tiny Aya Global cohere2 4/5 4/5 3/5^{⋆} text Tiny Aya Fire cohere2 3/5 4/5 3/5^{⋆} text Tiny Aya Water cohere2 4/5 4/5 3/5^{⋆} text Tiny Aya Earth cohere2 3/5 4/5 3/5^{⋆} text North Micro Vision Instruct cohere_compass 3/5 5/5, image PASS^{§‡}, image text only Aya Expanse 8B cohere 5/5 5/5 5/5 text Aya Expanse 32B cohere 5/5 5/5 5/5 text Aya Vision 8B aya_vision 5/5 5/5^{†}, image 5/5^{‡}, image text only Aya Vision 32B aya_vision 4/5 5/5^{†}, image PASS^{§‡}, image text only\nL426: A model can load across serving routes yet produce different evaluation outcomes. In one route, a special token left in the response causes an exact-match failure; another route strips it. This small probe checks runtime compatibility, not comparative model quality.\nL427: ### 8.5  Study 5: Retrieval and answer quality\nL428: A RAG assistant can fail in its retriever or in its generator, and the two need separate measurements. We answer six HotpotQA [cite136†46 ] distractor questions, each with ten candidate paragraphs of which two hold the supporting facts, using two retrievers, BM25 [cite137†36 ] and TF-IDF, that pass their top three paragraphs to the same generator (Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct).\nL429: Each sample carries the retrieved paragraphs as retrieval_context and the gold paragraphs as reference_contexts; RetrievalMetrics(k=3) scores what was retrieved, and quasi-exact match and token F1 score the answer. The prompt places the passages before the question.\nL430: Table 11: Retriever and answer metrics for HotpotQA ($N\\!=\\!6$, $k\\!=\\!3$, one shared generator).\nL431: Retriever  | Hit@3  | P@3  | R@3  | MRR  | nDCG@3  | QEM  | F1\nL432: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL433: BM25  | 0.833  | 0.500  | 0.750  | 0.750  | 0.704  | 0.333  | 0.370\nL434: TF-IDF  | 0.833  | 0.389  | 0.583  | 0.750  | 0.602  | 0.333  | 0.333\nL435: The retrievers tie on hit rate, but ranking-sensitive measures reveal differences in the retrieved evidence. Answer scores also change when the prompt places the question before rather than after the passages, while retrieval scores stay fixed. Six questions illustrate the measures rather than rank the retrievers; the case shows why retrieval and generation need separate measures.\nL436: ### 8.6  Study 6: Evaluating artifacts from the Lexsi Labs stack\nL437: \nL438: We test interoperability using sample artifacts from AgentTune (two trajectories), CuratorKIT, AlignTune, SafeTune, and CircuitKIT. The records are placeholders, so this study evaluates parsing and linking behavior, not model quality. An artifact-based link is checked against an identifier it contains; a caller-based link uses an identifier supplied externally.\nL439: Table 12: Evidence-linking outcomes for a sample AgentTune episode (constructed placeholder artifacts).\nL440: Evidence  | Granularity  | Ids given  | Result\nL441: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL442: AlignTune regression report  | aggregate  | artifact name, format  | joined (artifact)\nL443: AlignTune audit report  | aggregate  | checkpoint digest  | joined (caller)\nL444: CircuitKIT faithfulness report  | aggregate  | model digest  | joined (caller)\nL445: SafeTune audit event  | per event  | episode id, call id  | joined (caller)\nL446: SafeTune audit event  | per event  | decision only  | refused: describes, does not identify\nL447: CuratorKIT manifest  | aggregate  | row id  | refused: aggregate cannot pin one row\nL448: Final-state assertions verify the recorded episodes, while missing tool arguments and rewards remain unscored rather than inferred. Evidence records are joined only when their identifiers support the link; descriptive labels and aggregate manifests are refused when they cannot identify an episode.\nL449: ### 8.7  Study 7: Choosing a tool-calling model\n"
